<Project>

  <!--
    Convention: never bind a typed options class straight off an `IConfiguration configuration`
    parameter that was captured before `WebApplication.Build()` (e.g. the parameter passed into
    an `Add<X>Infrastructure(IConfiguration configuration)` extension method, called from
    Program.cs as `builder.Services.Add<X>Infrastructure(builder.Configuration)`).

    That snapshot does not see configuration added later — in particular, a
    WebApplicationFactory-based test's `ConfigureAppConfiguration` overrides (a real
    Testcontainers connection string/host/port) only land in the *fully built* configuration.
    Binding early doesn't throw or warn; it just silently keeps using the appsettings.json
    default, which can pass tests for the wrong reason (see CLAUDE.md, step 7, for exactly that
    happening with a leftover container masking the bug).

    Instead, resolve options lazily from the DI container's own IConfiguration, after Build():
      services.AddSingleton(sp => sp.GetRequiredService<IConfiguration>()
          .GetSection(FooOptions.SectionName).Get<FooOptions>()
          ?? throw new InvalidOperationException(...));
    or, for things that aren't plain singletons (AddDbContext, AddHttpClient, ...), use their
    (IServiceProvider, ...) configuration overload instead of the parameterless one.

    Each service's Infrastructure/ServiceCollectionExtensions.cs has a private
    `GetRequiredOptions<TOptions>` helper for this — reuse it there rather than writing the
    binding inline again. Hit three times (the orders/inventory connection string, the CI
    Testcontainers fixture, and RabbitMqOptions in both messaging services) before this comment
    existed — three strikes is a systemic gap, not bad luck.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
